.TH CCOL_SELECTABLE_FROM_CIRCQ 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_selectable_from_circq \- build a ccol_selectable from a ccol_circular_queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_selectable_from_circq(q, dir);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Builds a ccol_selectable value (a tagged union, here of type
ccol_selectable_circq) that wraps the given ccol_circular_queue pointer
with the given direction.

  ccol_select_read; wait for at least one message to arrive (read-wait)
  ccol_select_write; wait until the queue has room for a send, or until
                     its sends are turned off (write-wait)

The macro expands to a compound literal, so at run time it costs no
more than loading the pointer.

For either direction ccol_select() only reports readiness. It never
takes or reserves anything, so the caller has to make the call itself:
ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() when the read direction wins, and
ccol_circq_try_send_zc() when the write direction wins. Since nothing
is claimed or reserved, there is a time-of-check to time-of-use gap,
the same one as in POSIX select(2).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
q    ccol_circular_queue pointer to wrap. A NULL value makes a
     selectable that ccol_select() rejects with ccol_invalid_args.
dir  ccol_select_read or ccol_select_write.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_selectable value (not a pointer).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_circular_queue *q0 = ccol_circular_queue_create(8, NULL);
ccol_circular_queue *q1 = ccol_circular_queue_create(8, NULL);

size_t idx;
/* Read-wait on two queues: */
ccol_select_va(&idx,
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q1, ccol_select_read));
/* on ccol_success, claim the message with ccol_circq_try_recv_zc() on
   whichever of q0 and q1 idx points at */

/* Mixed: wait until q0 has room to send or q1 has a message: */
ccol_select_va(&idx,
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_write),
    ccol_selectable_from_circq(q1, ccol_select_read));

ccol_circular_queue_destroy(q0);
ccol_circular_queue_destroy(q1);
.fi
